<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zagros Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zagros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zagros Mountains are a long mountain range in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, stretching from the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and separating the Mesopotamian plain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a>. Their highland peoples repeatedly pressed on Mesopotamian states, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>. The Zagros was the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, and its tribes, including the Zand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lak">Lak</a>, formed the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains.jpg" alt="Zagros Mountains" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terpsichores · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ecoregion_PA0446.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The military threat came from the Gutians, a highland people from the <b>Zagros</b> mountains, whose incursions into the Mesopotamian lowlands intensified under Shar-Kali-Sharri, disrupting agriculture and trade while Akkadian armies were tied down in reactive punitive campaigns.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The first phase of territorial ambition came under the Amorite conqueror Shamshi-Adad I (r. c. 1808–1776 BCE), who seized Assur, styled himself &quot;King of the Universe&quot; and built a kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia stretching from the <b>Zagros</b> foothills to the Euphrates and Mari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1217 Muhammad II marched on Baghdad against the Abbasid caliph, but the campaign was abandoned when snow overwhelmed his army in the <b>Zagros</b> mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ishpuini and grandson Menua began writing in Urartian, a language related to Hurrian, using a cuneiform script adapted from Assyria, and extended royal power to the religious centre of Musasir in the <b>Zagros</b>, to the lands north of Lake Van and towards Lake Urmia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-urartu">Kingdom of Urartu · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Median Empire emerged in the 7th century BCE from a confederation of tribes in the <b>Zagros Mountains</b>, many of whose chiefdoms had long paid tribute to Assyria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Shulgi, the army undertook repeated campaigns in the <b>Zagros</b> against peoples such as the Lullubi and the city of Simurrum, while Susa and parts of Elam were brought under Ur’s control.</blockquote><a href="/empire/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
